Given, \[v(t)=2+\cos\left(\omega t+\frac{\pi}{6}\right)\] The average value is equal to the DC component: \[V_{\mathrm{avg}}=2\,\text{V} \tag{i}\] The rms value is \[V_{\mathrm{rms}}
=
\sqrt{2^2+\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)^2}
=
\sqrt{4+\frac{1}{2}}
=
\sqrt{\frac{9}{2}}
\tag{ii}\] From (i) and (ii), \[\frac{V_{\mathrm{rms}}}{V_{\mathrm{avg}}}
=
\frac{\sqrt{9/2}}{2}
=
\frac{3}{2\sqrt{2}}\] \[\boxed{\text{A) $\dfrac{3}{2\sqrt{2}}$}}\]

Given that the bridge is balanced. <img src="img/1f34e2aeeac10da7.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,320px)"> For a balanced bridge, \[Z_{AD}Z_{BC}=Z_{AB}Z_{CD}\] \[1\,\text{k}\Omega \times 2\,\text{k}\Omega
=
Z_{AB}\times 4\,\text{k}\Omega\] \[Z_{AB}=0.5\,\text{k}\Omega\] Current through branch \(AB\) is \[I_{AB}
=
\frac{V_{AB}}{Z_{AB}}
=
\frac{2}{0.5\times10^3}
=
4\,\text{mA}
\tag{i}\] <img src="img/936f37dfa854cfe2.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,320px)"> Using current division, \[I_{AB}
=
I\,
\frac{1\,\text{k}\Omega+4\,\text{k}\Omega}
{1\,\text{k}\Omega+4\,\text{k}\Omega+0.5\,\text{k}\Omega+2\,\text{k}\Omega}\] \[I_{AB}
=
I\left(\frac{5}{7.5}\right)\] Using (i), \[4\,\text{mA}
=
I\left(\frac{5}{7.5}\right)\] \[I=6\,\text{mA}\] \[\boxed{\text{D) 6 mA }}\]

Given circuit is shown below. <img src="img/2db546acb67715ee.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)"> Voltage across \(R\) is given by, \[V_0(t)=V_0(\infty)+\big[V_0(0^+)-V_0(\infty)\big]e^{-t/\tau}
\tag{i}\] <ol><li><strong>At \(t=0^-/t&lt;0\)/steady state :</strong><br/>In steady state, capacitor behaves as an open circuit. <img src="img/248b64fc7bafa550.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,205px)"> From figure, \[V_0(0^-)=IR=0\times R=0~\text{V}\] \[V_C(0^-)=1~\text{V}\] From the property of capacitor, \[V_C(0^-)=V_C(0^+)=1~\text{V}\]</li><li><strong>At \(t=0^+\) :</strong><br/><img src="img/d29f8af6bda51657.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,205px)"> \[V_0(0^+)=-1~\text{V}
\tag{ii}\] For an RC network, \[\tau=RC\]</li><li><strong>At \(t=\infty\)/steady state :</strong><br/>In steady state, capacitor behaves as an open circuit. <img src="img/29e61fe4858e5cd9.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,205px)"> \[V_0(\infty)=0~\text{V}
\tag{iii}\]</li></ol> Substituting (ii) and (iii) into (i), \[V_0(t)=0+\big[-1-0\big]e^{-t/RC}\] \[V_0(t)=-e^{-t/RC}\] Hence, \[\boxed{\text{(B)}\;-e^{-t/RC}}\]

The given circuit is an inverting amplifier. With \(V_{i}=0\), the output is produced only due to the input offset voltage. \[\text{Noise Gain}=1+\frac{R_{f}}{R_{in}}
=1+\frac{10\,\text{k}\Omega}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}
=11\] Given, \[V_{OS}=10\,\text{mV}\] Hence, \[V_{o}=\text{Noise Gain}\times V_{OS}
=11\times10\,\text{mV}
=110\,\text{mV}\] \[\boxed{\text{(d) }110\,\text{mV}}\]

A unity gain buffer has a low-frequency voltage gain of unity. At the bandwidth (cut-off frequency), the voltage gain falls to \(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\) of its low-frequency value (i.e., \(-3\,\text{dB}\) point). Given, \[A_{v}=1,\qquad V_{in}=2\,\text{V},\qquad f=f_{BW}=1\,\text{MHz}\] Hence, the output voltage is \[V_{o}=A_{v}\times\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\times V_{in}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\times2
=\frac{2}{\sqrt{2}}\,\text{V}\] \[\boxed{\textbf{(c) } \dfrac{2}{\sqrt{2}}\,\text{V}}\]
